# udev rules for the SyncAI robot's hot-pluggable peripherals.
#
# udev runs on the HOST, not in the robot container — install these there:
#
#   sudo cp src/syncai_sys_manager/udev/99-syncai-devices.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload
#   sudo udevadm trigger
#
# The robot container gets the raw /dev/videoN nodes via docker-compose device
# passthrough; the /dev/syncai/* symlinks created here live on the host and are
# what the compose file (and any host-side tooling) should reference, so the
# passthrough no longer depends on kernel enumeration order.

# ── Cameras ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two TechNexion UVC cameras. As of 2026-08 both units on the robot are the
# SAME model — VCS-AR0234-C, 3407:57d1 — so neither the product ID nor any
# other USB descriptor field except the serial can tell them apart. The match
# below is therefore on ATTRS{serial}:
#
#   0000000014C4006204441280C7106134 → camera0   (today's /dev/video0, port 2-3.1)
#   000000001056074A0551C45186388248 → camera1   (today's /dev/video2, port 2-3.4)
#
# The camera0/camera1 assignment is arbitrary between two identical sensors;
# it was chosen to keep camera0 pointing at the unit it already resolved to
# (the one on 2-3.1), so `publish_camera*.sh` and bringup's device_index 0
# keep addressing the same physical camera as before.
#
# This file has now been wrong twice about how to tell these two apart, so both
# dead ends are recorded here rather than rediscovered a third time:
#
#   1. KERNELS=="2-3.1" / "2-3.4" (USB port path). Matched nothing after the
#      cameras were re-cabled off the hub, and a port path is not the property
#      we care about — it changes when someone moves a plug.
#   2. ATTRS{idProduct}=="57d1" / "0144" (per-model, on the assumption the
#      second unit was a VCI-AR0144-C). The 0144 rule matched nothing because
#      no such device is on the bus, and — the quieter half of the bug — the
#      57d1 rule then matched BOTH cameras, so /dev/video0 and /dev/video2 each
#      claimed `syncai/camera0`. udev hands a contested symlink to exactly one
#      device, so camera0 still appeared and looked healthy while silently
#      pointing at whichever unit won that race. Two rules that can match the
#      same node are the failure mode to watch for here, not just a rule that
#      matches none.
#
# Serials survive both a re-cable and a re-enumeration; they do NOT survive
# swapping in a replacement camera, which is the one maintenance event that
# requires editing this file. Read the new serial off
# `udevadm info -q property -n /dev/videoN | grep ID_SERIAL_SHORT`.
#
# If the fleet ever does carry two different models again, matching on
# idProduct is fine for that pair — but keep one rule per physical camera, so
# no two rules can claim the same SYMLINK name.
#
# Each UVC camera exposes TWO video4linux nodes: index 0 is the actual video
# capture node, index 1 is a UVC metadata node that produces no frames. That
# is why /dev/video1 and /dev/video3 exist but are useless as cameras — the
# ATTR{index}=="0" match below skips the metadata nodes so the symlinks can
# never land on one.
#
# The old `camera_ar0234` / `camera_ar0144` model aliases are gone. They existed
# so a consumer could ask for a sensor by model when the two differed in max
# resolution and frame rate; with two identical units the name no longer
# selects anything, and `camera_ar0234` was in fact the second contested
# symlink of dead end 2 above. Restore per-model aliases along with a mixed
# pair, not before.
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTR{index}=="0", ATTRS{idVendor}=="3407", ATTRS{idProduct}=="57d1", ATTRS{serial}=="0000000014C4006204441280C7106134", SYMLINK+="syncai/camera0"
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTR{index}=="0", ATTRS{idVendor}=="3407", ATTRS{idProduct}=="57d1", ATTRS{serial}=="000000001056074A0551C45186388248", SYMLINK+="syncai/camera1"

# ── Speaker ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# USB audio dongle (Jieli CD002-AUDIO, e2b8:0811). Its ALSA card number is
# whatever enumerates after the Tegra HDA/APE cards (card 2 today) and is not
# guaranteed stable, but the card *name* "CD002AUDIO" is derived from the USB
# descriptor and is — so audio consumers (e.g. the backend TTS gateway) should
# open it as ALSA device "hw:CARD=CD002AUDIO" / "plughw:CARD=CD002AUDIO"
# rather than by number. The symlinks below exist for presence detection and
# for recovering the current card number without parsing aplay output: the
# link target's name embeds it (controlC<N> / pcmC<N>D0p).
SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="controlC[0-9]*", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="e2b8", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0811", SYMLINK+="syncai/speaker"
SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="pcmC[0-9]*D0p", SUBSYSTEMS=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="e2b8", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0811", SYMLINK+="syncai/speaker_pcm"
